<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">

    <h1>Student Discipline, Manifestation Determinations, & Due Process in District 15 (Park Slope)</h1>

    <p>District 15, which includes vibrant neighborhoods like Park Slope, Windsor Terrace, and Sunset Park, strives to balance student safety and well-being with the legal rights afforded to all students, especially those with disabilities. This guide provides a comprehensive overview of disciplinary procedures, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process rights within District 15, aligning with New York State Education Department (NYSED) regulations and the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).</p>

    <h2>Understanding Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs)</h2>

    <p>A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a critical process mandated by the IDEA (20 U.S.C. § 1415(k)) when a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 plan faces disciplinary action that could result in a suspension exceeding 10 consecutive school days, or a pattern of removals that constitute a change of placement. A "change of placement" can also occur through shorter suspensions if they accumulate and create a pattern of exclusion from the student's regular educational setting. (34 CFR § 300.536). The MDR is designed to determine if the student's misconduct is a direct result of their disability or if it’s a consequence of the school's failure to properly implement the student's IEP or 504 Plan. This review must occur *no more than* 10 school days after the decision to change the student’s placement because of a violation of a code of student conduct.</p>

    <h3>Who Participates in an MDR?</h3>

    <p>The MDR team *must* include the following:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Representatives of the school district (e.g., principal, special education administrator)</li>
        <li>The student's parent(s) or legal guardian(s)</li>
        <li>Relevant members of the IEP team (as determined by the parent and the school district). This should include individuals knowledgeable about the student, the student’s disability, the relevant evaluation data, and the student’s IEP.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide</h3>

    <ol>
        <li><b>Notification:</b> The school must promptly notify parents of the disciplinary action and the need for an MDR. This notification should be in writing and clearly explain the purpose of the review and the parent's rights.</li>
        <li><b>Record Review:</b> The MDR team reviews all relevant information in the student's file. This includes the IEP or 504 plan, any teacher observations, and any relevant information provided by the parents. Specifically, the team must consider:
            <ul>
                <li>The student’s IEP</li>
                <li>Any teacher observations</li>
                <li>Any relevant information provided by the parents.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><b>Determining Manifestation:</b> The team addresses two key questions:
            <ul>
                <li>Was the conduct in question caused by, or did it have a direct and substantial relationship to, the child's disability?</li>
                <li>Was the conduct in question the direct result of the school district's failure to implement the IEP?</li>
            </ul>
            <p>If the answer to *either* of these questions is "yes," then the conduct is deemed a manifestation of the student's disability.</p>
        </li>
        <li><b>Outcomes Based on Determination:</b>
            <ul>
                <li><b>If the conduct is a manifestation:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li>The IEP team *must* conduct a functional behavioral assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted prior to the behavior that led to the discipline.</li>
                        <li>Implement a behavior intervention plan (BIP) for the student, unless one already exists. If a BIP already exists, it needs to be reviewed and modified, if necessary, to address the behavior.</li>
                        <li>The student must be returned to the placement from which they were removed, unless the parent and the school district agree to a change of placement as part of the modification of the IEP.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li><b>If the conduct is NOT a manifestation:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li>The student may be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities, *except* that the school district must still provide services to the child during the period of removal, consistent with 34 CFR §300.530(d).</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li><b>Documentation:</b> The outcome of the MDR, including the rationale behind the decision, must be documented in writing. Parents are entitled to a copy of this documentation.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Parent Advocacy Tips for MDRs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Prepare Thoroughly:</b> Gather all relevant information about your child's disability, IEP/504 plan, and any incidents leading to the disciplinary action. Document everything.</li>
        <li><b>Actively Participate:</b> Ask clarifying questions, share your insights about your child's behavior, and advocate for appropriate supports and interventions.</li>
        <li><b>Know Your Rights:</b> Familiarize yourself with IDEA regulations regarding MDRs and disciplinary procedures.</li>
        <li><b>Seek Outside Support:</b> Consult with a special education attorney or advocate for guidance and representation.</li>
        <li><b>Challenge the Decision:</b> If you disagree with the outcome of the MDR, you have the right to challenge the decision through due process.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Common District Pitfalls to Watch Out For</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Predetermination:</b> The school has already decided the outcome of the MDR before the meeting even takes place.</li>
        <li><b>Insufficient Information:</b> The MDR team lacks crucial information about the student's disability or IEP.</li>
        <li><b>Ignoring Parent Input:</b> The school disregards the parent's perspective and insights.</li>
        <li><b>Failure to Implement IEP:</b> The disciplinary action stems from the school's failure to properly implement the student's IEP, but the MDR team fails to acknowledge this.</li>
        <li><b>Lack of Qualified Personnel:</b> The MDR team lacks members with sufficient knowledge about the student's disability and the requirements of IDEA.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Suspensions in District 15: Your Rights and Recourse</h2>

    <p>Suspensions, especially long-term suspensions, can significantly disrupt a student's education and well-being. Understanding your rights and the procedures surrounding suspensions is paramount.</p>

    <h3>Types of Suspensions</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>Short-term Suspension:</b> Typically less than 5 school days.</li>
        <li><b>Long-term Suspension:</b> More than 5 school days.</li>
        <li><b>Superintendent's Suspension:</b> A very serious disciplinary action that can lead to removal from the school district. This requires a formal hearing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Due Process Rights During Suspensions</h3>

    <p>Students facing suspension have fundamental due process rights guaranteed by the Fourteenth Amendment of the U.S. Constitution and New York State Education Law § 3214. These rights ensure fairness and protect students from arbitrary disciplinary actions.</p>

    <ol>
        <li><b>Notice of Charges:</b> You have the right to be informed, in writing, of the specific charges against your child. The notice should be clear, detailed, and provide sufficient information for you to prepare a defense. (NYSED Part 100.2(b)(4)).</li>
        <li><b>Opportunity to be Heard:</b> You and your child have the right to a hearing or meeting with school officials to present your side of the story and challenge the allegations. This is your opportunity to present evidence, witnesses, and argue against the suspension. For suspensions exceeding five days, a more formal hearing is required.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Representation:</b> You have the right to be represented by an attorney or advocate during the suspension process, especially for long-term suspensions or Superintendent's hearings.</li>
        <li><b>Impartial Decision-Maker:</b> The decision to suspend your child must be made by an impartial decision-maker who is not biased against your child.</li>
        <li><b>Right to Appeal:</b> If you believe the suspension is unfair or violates your child's rights, you have the right to appeal the decision to a higher authority within the school district. The specific appeal process will vary depending on the length of the suspension.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Challenging a Suspension: A Step-by-Step Guide</h3>

    <ol>
        <li><b>Review the Suspension Notice:</b> Carefully examine the suspension notice for any errors, inconsistencies, or lack of detail.</li>
        <li><b>Gather Evidence:</b> Collect any evidence that supports your child's defense, such as witness statements, documents, or photographs.</li>
        <li><b>Attend the Hearing/Meeting:</b> Actively participate in the hearing or meeting, present your evidence, and challenge the allegations.</li>
        <li><b>Consult with an Attorney/Advocate:</b> Seek legal advice from a special education attorney or advocate, especially if the suspension is lengthy or complex.</li>
        <li><b>File an Appeal:</b> If you are not satisfied with the outcome of the hearing/meeting, file a formal appeal with the appropriate school district official. Follow the district's specific appeal procedures.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>FAQ: Discipline and Special Education in District 15</h3>

    <dl>
        <dt>Q: What is the "10-day rule" in special education discipline?</dt>
        <dd>A: The "10-day rule" refers to the point at which a series of suspensions can trigger special education protections, specifically the need for a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR). If a student with an IEP or 504 plan is removed from their current placement for more than 10 *cumulative* school days in a single school year, the district must determine if the series of removals constitutes a change of placement. If it does, an MDR is required.</dd>

        <dt>Q: My child was suspended for behavior related to their disability. What should I do?</dt>
        <dd>A: Immediately request a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR). Ensure the team reviews your child's IEP and determines if the behavior was a manifestation of their disability. If it was, the IEP must be revised to address the behavior. If the school fails to conduct an MDR, you have grounds to file a complaint or request a due process hearing.</dd>

        <dt>Q: Can my child be expelled from school if they have an IEP?</dt>
        <dd>A: While expulsion is possible, it's rare for students with IEPs. The MDR process and IDEA provide significant protections. Before expulsion can be considered, the school must conduct an MDR and ensure that the student's behavior is not a manifestation of their disability. Even if the behavior is not a manifestation, the school must continue to provide educational services during the expulsion period. A Superintendent's Hearing is generally required before expulsion. </dd>

        <dt>Q: What if my child's IEP isn't being followed, and that's contributing to their behavior problems?</dt>
        <dd>A: This is a critical issue. Document all instances where the IEP is not being implemented. Bring this to the attention of the school principal, special education director, and IEP team immediately. Request an IEP meeting to address the implementation issues and revise the IEP if necessary. If the IEP implementation issues are contributing to the behavior, this should be a key argument during the MDR.</dd>

        <dt>Q: What resources are available to help me advocate for my child during disciplinary proceedings?</dt>
        <dd>A: Consider contacting a special education attorney or advocate in New York City. Organizations like INCLUDEnyc and the New York Legal Assistance Group (NYLAG) may also provide assistance. You can also contact the District 15 Special Education Parent Teacher Association (SEPTA) for support and guidance.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Key Contacts and Resources</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><b>District 15 Superintendent's Office:</b> (Find contact information on the NYC Department of Education website)</li>
        <li><b>Your Child's School Principal and Guidance Counselor:</b> They are your first point of contact for addressing disciplinary concerns.</li>
        <li><b>INCLUDEnyc:</b> A non-profit organization providing information and advocacy services for families of children with disabilities in New York City. (<a href="https://www.includenyc.org/" target="_blank">https://www.includenyc.org/</a>)</li>
        <li><b>New York Legal Assistance Group (NYLAG):</b> Provides free legal services to low-income New Yorkers, including special education cases. (<a href="https://www.nylag.org/" target="_blank">https://www.nylag.org/</a>)</li>
        <li><b>NYSED Office of Special Education:</b> For information on special education laws and regulations. (<a href="http://www.nysed.gov/specialed/" target="_blank">http://www.nysed.gov/specialed/</a>)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Navigating student discipline, especially when a disability is involved, can be overwhelming. By understanding your rights, actively participating in the process, and seeking support when needed, you can ensure your child receives a fair and appropriate education.</p>

</div>

